    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. checking liveness probes now. i'll look at the deployment yaml. i'm also seeing some intermittent network timeouts in the logs right before the restart. could that be related to the probe failing if the app is still technically running? also what kind of kubectl commands should i use to see if the probe is actually the thing triggering the restart?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Network timeouts can be a red herring, but they can indeed be related to the liveness probe. If the app is still running, but the probe is failing, it can cause the pod to restart. This is because the liveness probe is essentially saying, \"Hey, I haven't seen any signs of life from this pod in a while, let's restart it and try again.\"\n\nTo investigate the liveness probe, you can use the following `kubectl` commands:\n\n- `kubectl get deployments -o yaml` to inspect the `Deployment` YAML file and verify the liveness probe configuration.\n- `kubectl get pods -o yaml` to inspect the pod's configuration and verify the liveness probe settings.\n- `kubectl logs -f <pod_name>` to tail the pod's logs and see if there are any errors or warnings related to the liveness probe.\n- `kubectl describe pod <pod_name>` to get a detailed view of the pod's status, including any errors or warnings related to the liveness probe.\n\nTo see if the probe is actually the thing triggering the restart, you can use the following command:\n\n- `kubectl describe pod <pod_name> | grep restart` to see if the pod's restart is being",
      "source": "target",
